from .lg import S
PROBLEMS = [
(47, ("(JEE Advanced, integer) Eight reactions are listed: (a) CHCl<sub>3</sub> + KOC(CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>3</sub>; (b) CH<sub>2</sub>N<sub>2</sub>, hν; (c) CH<sub>2</sub>I<sub>2</sub> + Zn(Cu) in ether; (d) CH<sub>2</sub>=C=O, hν; (e) 3-phenyl-3-(trifluoromethyl)diazirine, hν; (f) CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>Br + NaOEt, EtOH; (g) Ph<sub>3</sub>P=CH<sub>2</sub> in THF; (h) the sodium salt of benzaldehyde tosylhydrazone heated in diglyme (aprotic). In how many of these is a carbene or a carbenoid (a species that transfers CH<sub>2</sub>/CR<sub>2</sub> like a carbene) generated as the reactive intermediate?",
      None, "6 (a, b, c, d, e, h)",
      "(a) α-Elimination of HCl from CCl<sub>3</sub><sup>−</sup> gives :CCl<sub>2</sub>. (b) CH<sub>2</sub>N<sub>2</sub> loses N<sub>2</sub> to give :CH<sub>2</sub>. (c) ICH<sub>2</sub>ZnI is a zinc carbenoid. (d) Ketene photolysis expels CO to give :CH<sub>2</sub>. (e) Diazirine photolysis loses N<sub>2</sub> to give Ph(CF<sub>3</sub>)C:. (h) The Bamford–Stevens aprotic pathway gives the diazo compound PhCHN<sub>2</sub> and then PhCH:. (f) is a plain E2 (no carbene) and (g) is a phosphorus ylide, which is a stable carbanion-type reagent and not a free carbene. Count = 6 ⚑ (if only free carbenes are counted, (c) is excluded and the answer is 5).")),
(48, ("(JEE Advanced, integer) <i>cis</i>-But-2-ene is allowed to react with methylene (:CH<sub>2</sub>) to give 1,2-dimethylcyclopropanes. Let <i>x</i> be the total number of distinct stereoisomers of 1,2-dimethylcyclopropane that can form when only singlet :CH<sub>2</sub> reacts, and <i>y</i> the number that can form when the reacting species is triplet :CH<sub>2</sub>. Give <i>x</i> and <i>y</i>. (Count each enantiomer separately.)",
      S([("cis (meso)", "C[C@H]1C[C@H]1C"), ("trans (one enantiomer)", "C[C@H]1C[C@@H]1C")]),
      "x = 1, y = 3",
      "Singlet carbene adds in a single concerted step with retention of alkene geometry (Skell): cis-butene gives only cis-1,2-dimethylcyclopropane, which is meso, so x = 1. Triplet carbene adds one bond at a time to give a 1,3-diradical; ring closure requires spin inversion, which is slower than rotation about the former C=C bond, so both cis (meso) and trans products form. The trans compound is chiral and forms as a racemate (two enantiomers), so y = 1 + 2 = 3.")),
(49, ("(JEE Advanced, integer) Racemic 4-methylcyclohexene is treated with CHCl<sub>3</sub>, 50% aq. NaOH and a catalytic amount of benzyltriethylammonium chloride. The product is a 7,7-dichloronorcarane. How many stereoisomers (counting enantiomers separately) of the product can be formed? Why is the quaternary ammonium salt needed?",
      S([("4-methylcyclohexene", "CC1CCC=CC1"), ("product", "CC1CCC2C(C1)C2(Cl)Cl")]),
      "4 stereoisomers (two diastereomers, each as a pair of enantiomers)",
      "The product is 7,7-dichloro-3-methylbicyclo[4.1.0]heptane. :CCl<sub>2</sub> adds syn to the C=C, so the ring fusion is necessarily cis (C1 and C6 are fixed relative to each other: 2 possibilities). C3 (bearing CH<sub>3</sub>) is a third stereocentre, so there are 2 × 2 = 4 stereoisomers; C7 is not a stereocentre because it carries two identical Cl. Attack from the face syn or anti to the CH<sub>3</sub> gives the two diastereomers, and because the alkene is racemic each diastereomer is racemic (face selectivity is poor because CH<sub>3</sub> is remote; the ratio is close to 1:1 ⚑). The ammonium salt transports HO<sup>−</sup>/CCl<sub>3</sub><sup>−</sup> ion pairs across the phase boundary; the interfacial CCl<sub>3</sub><sup>−</sup> expels Cl<sup>−</sup> in the organic phase, so water-sensitive :CCl<sub>2</sub> is generated next to the alkene and is not hydrolysed.")),
(50, ("(Assertion–Reason) For each pair choose: (a) A and R true, R explains A; (b) A and R true, R does not explain A; (c) A true, R false; (d) A false, R true.<br>"
       "<b>Pair 1.</b> A: Photolysis of diazomethane in <i>cis</i>-but-2-ene diluted with a large excess of an inert gas at high pressure gives progressively less stereospecific cyclopropanation. R: Collisions deactivate the initially formed singlet :CH<sub>2</sub> to the triplet state, which adds in a stepwise manner.<br>"
       "<b>Pair 2.</b> A: The ground state of methylene, :CH<sub>2</sub>, is the singlet. R: The singlet–triplet energy gap in :CH<sub>2</sub> is about 9 kcal mol<sup>−1</sup>.<br>"
       "<b>Pair 3.</b> A: Dichlorocarbene adds to <i>trans</i>-but-2-ene to give <i>trans</i>-1,1-dichloro-2,3-dimethylcyclopropane stereospecifically. R: Ground-state :CCl<sub>2</sub> is a triplet.<br>"
       "<b>Pair 4.</b> A: Triplet carbenes react with alkenes through diradical intermediates. R: Triplet :CH<sub>2</sub> is paramagnetic and can be detected by EPR.",
      None, "Pair 1: (a); Pair 2: (d); Pair 3: (c); Pair 4: (b)",
      "Pair 1: true. Singlet :CH<sub>2</sub> formed from CH<sub>2</sub>N<sub>2</sub> is relaxed by collisions to the lower-lying triplet (intersystem crossing), whose stepwise addition scrambles geometry, so R explains A. Pair 2: A is false because the ground state is triplet (<sup>3</sup>B<sub>1</sub>); R is true (the singlet lies about 9 kcal mol<sup>−1</sup> higher). Pair 3: A is true (singlet :CCl<sub>2</sub> is stabilised by π-donation from the Cl lone pairs into the empty p orbital and adds concertedly), but R is false because :CCl<sub>2</sub> has a singlet ground state. Pair 4: both statements are true, but paramagnetism is a property of the spin state and does not explain the mechanism of addition.")),
(51, ("(CSIR-NET style) <i>p</i>-Cresol is heated with CHCl<sub>3</sub> and aqueous NaOH (Reimer–Tiemann). Give (i) the normal product and (ii) the abnormal product that is formed because the para position is blocked, and explain why the second product cannot rearomatise.",
      S([("normal", "Cc1ccc(O)c(C=O)c1"), ("abnormal", "O=C1C=CC(C)(C(Cl)Cl)C=C1")]),
      "2-hydroxy-5-methylbenzaldehyde (normal); 4-(dichloromethyl)-4-methylcyclohexa-2,5-dienone (abnormal)",
      "The phenoxide (ortho/para nucleophile) attacks electrophilic :CCl<sub>2</sub>. Ortho attack, proton loss and hydrolysis of the ArCHCl<sub>2</sub>/ArCH(OH)Cl species give salicylaldehyde-type product 2-hydroxy-5-methylbenzaldehyde. Attack of the phenoxide at C-4 (the carbon bearing CH<sub>3</sub>) gives a cyclohexadienone carrying CH<sub>3</sub> and CHCl<sub>2</sub> on the same sp<sup>3</sup> carbon. That carbon has no hydrogen to lose, so the ring cannot regain aromaticity; the cross-conjugated dienone is isolated (the dichloromethyl group is only slowly hydrolysed).")),
(52, ("(JEE Advanced, integer) The carbylamine (isocyanide) test uses CHCl<sub>3</sub> and alcoholic KOH. How many of the following amines give a positive test: CH<sub>3</sub>NH<sub>2</sub>, (CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>2</sub>NH, C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>NH<sub>2</sub>, (CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>3</sub>CNH<sub>2</sub>, (C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>)<sub>3</sub>N, C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>NHCH<sub>3</sub>? Write the key steps for aniline.",
      None, "3 (CH<sub>3</sub>NH<sub>2</sub>, C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>NH<sub>2</sub>, (CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>3</sub>CNH<sub>2</sub>)",
      "Only primary amines can lose two N–H protons (overall two HCl) to reach the isocyanide R–N<sup>+</sup>≡C<sup>−</sup>. Steps for aniline: KOH + CHCl<sub>3</sub> → :CCl<sub>2</sub>; PhNH<sub>2</sub> attacks the carbene to give PhNH<sub>2</sub><sup>+</sup>–CCl<sub>2</sub><sup>−</sup>, then PhNH–CHCl<sub>2</sub>; base removes HCl twice (N–H and C–H with chloride loss) to give PhNC (foul odour). Secondary and tertiary amines (N–H count insufficient) do not give isocyanides; <i>tert</i>-butylamine does, because the reaction occurs at N and not at the alkyl carbon.")),
(53, ("(IChO-style) Geraniol, (<i>E</i>)-3,7-dimethylocta-2,6-dien-1-ol, has one allylic and one remote trisubstituted C=C. Predict the main product with (i) 1.0 equiv Et<sub>2</sub>Zn / CH<sub>2</sub>I<sub>2</sub> (Furukawa) in CH<sub>2</sub>Cl<sub>2</sub>, and (ii) 1.0 equiv <i>m</i>-CPBA. State the stereochemical consequence of (i) for the substituents on the new ring, and what changes with 2.5 equiv of the zinc reagent.",
      S([("geraniol", "CC(C)=CCC/C(C)=C/CO"), ("(i) Zn carbenoid", "CC(C)=CCCC1(C)CC1CO"), ("(ii) m-CPBA", "CC1(C)OC1CCC(C)=CCO")]),
      "(i) cyclopropanation of the C2=C3 (allylic) alkene; (ii) epoxidation of the C6=C7 alkene",
      "(i) The zinc carbenoid is delivered by coordination to the alkoxide/OH oxygen, so the proximal C2=C3 bond reacts even though the remote C6=C7 is more electron-rich. The cyclopropanation is syn and stereospecific, so the <i>E</i> geometry is kept: CH<sub>2</sub>OH ends up cis to CH<sub>3</sub> and trans to the C4 chain. Product: [2-methyl-2-(4-methylpent-3-enyl)cyclopropyl]methanol. (ii) <i>m</i>-CPBA is an electrophilic oxidant with no directing effect; the allylic C2=C3 is deactivated inductively by the OH, so the more electron-rich C6=C7 is epoxidised preferentially (not exclusively ⚑). With 2.5 equiv of the zinc reagent both alkenes are cyclopropanated (the second more slowly, undirected).")),
(54, ("(IChO-style) (<i>S</i>)-2-Methylbutanoic acid is converted into its homologue by: (1) SOCl<sub>2</sub>; (2) excess CH<sub>2</sub>N<sub>2</sub> (≥ 2 equiv); (3) Ag<sub>2</sub>O (or PhCO<sub>2</sub>Ag/Et<sub>3</sub>N), H<sub>2</sub>O, warm. Give the product with its CIP descriptor, name the key intermediate in step 3, and explain (a) why 2 equiv of CH<sub>2</sub>N<sub>2</sub> are needed and (b) what happens to the stereocentre.",
      S([("starting acid", "CC[C@H](C)C(=O)O"), ("product", "CC[C@H](C)CC(=O)O")]),
      "(<i>S</i>)-3-methylpentanoic acid; intermediate: ketene (from Wolff rearrangement)",
      "Step 2: the acid chloride reacts with CH<sub>2</sub>N<sub>2</sub> to give the α-diazoketone RCOCHN<sub>2</sub>; the second equivalent of CH<sub>2</sub>N<sub>2</sub> destroys the HCl formed (giving CH<sub>3</sub>Cl + N<sub>2</sub>), otherwise HCl would convert the diazoketone to RCOCH<sub>2</sub>Cl. Step 3: Ag(I) promotes loss of N<sub>2</sub> to a silver carbenoid, and Wolff rearrangement (1,2-shift of R to the carbenoid carbon) gives the ketene R–CH=C=O, which water captures to give RCH<sub>2</sub>CO<sub>2</sub>H. The migrating carbon moves with retention of configuration. The spatial order of groups is unchanged and the CIP priorities (CH<sub>2</sub>CO<sub>2</sub>H &gt; C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub> &gt; CH<sub>3</sub> &gt; H) follow the same order as before (CO<sub>2</sub>H &gt; C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub> &gt; CH<sub>3</sub> &gt; H), so the descriptor stays S.")),
(55, ("(IChO-style) (a) Ethyl diazoacetate and benzene with Rh<sub>2</sub>(OAc)<sub>4</sub> give a C<sub>9</sub>H<sub>10</sub>O<sub>2</sub> product through a norcaradiene intermediate: name the major isomer and the process. (b) 1-Diazo-4-phenylbutan-2-one, PhCH<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>COCHN<sub>2</sub>, with Cu or Rh catalyst gives a bicyclic C<sub>10</sub>H<sub>10</sub>O ketone: draw it and give the ring sizes. (c) Which type of C-7 substituent shifts the norcaradiene ⇌ cycloheptatriene equilibrium toward norcaradiene? Name one example.",
      S([("(b) substrate", "O=C(C[N+]#N)CCc1ccccc1".replace("C[N+]#N", "C=[N+]=[N-]")), ("(b) product", "O=C1CCC2=CC=CC=CC12")]),
      "(a) ethyl cyclohepta-2,4,6-triene-1-carboxylate (Buchner ring expansion); (b) a 5–7 fused hydroazulenone; (c) π-acceptors (e.g. 7,7-dicyano)",
      "(a) The rhodium carbenoid adds to one C=C of benzene to give a norcaradiene (bicyclo[4.1.0]hepta-2,4-diene), which undergoes a rapid, reversible electrocyclic ring opening (6π, disrotatory) to the cycloheptatriene; the ester at C-7 gives ethyl cycloheptatriene-7-carboxylate (C<sub>9</sub>H<sub>10</sub>O<sub>2</sub>). (b) Intramolecular Buchner reaction: the carbene carbon adds across the ipso/ortho bond (giving a 5-membered ketone ring: carbene C, C=O, CH<sub>2</sub>, CH<sub>2</sub>, ipso C); ring opening of the norcaradiene gives a cycloheptatriene fused to cyclopentanone (bicyclo[5.3.0]decatrienone). (c) π-acceptor substituents (CN, CO<sub>2</sub>R when doubled up) at C-7 lower the Walsh orbital energy of the cyclopropane and stabilise the norcaradiene; 7,7-dicyanonorcaradiene is the observed form.")),
(56, ("(IChO-style) Starting from 2,3-dimethylbut-2-ene: (i) CHBr<sub>3</sub>, KOC(CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>3</sub>; (ii) CH<sub>3</sub>Li, −78 °C → 25 °C (Doering–LaFlamme). Give the final product, its number of <sup>13</sup>C NMR signals, and the intermediates. Then say what is formed from <i>cis</i>-cyclooctene by the same two steps and why that product is chiral.",
      S([("final product 1", "CC(C)=C=C(C)C"), ("final product 2", "C1CCCC=C=CC1".replace("C1CCCC=C=CC1", "C1CCCCC=C=C1"))]),
      "tetramethylallene (3 <sup>13</sup>C signals); 1,2-cyclononadiene (chiral allene)",
      "Step (i): dibromocarbene cyclopropanation gives 1,1-dibromo-2,2,3,3-tetramethylcyclopropane. Step (ii): Br/Li exchange gives a cyclopropylidene carbenoid, which loses LiBr with concerted electrocyclic ring opening (cleavage of the C2–C3 bond opposite to the carbenoid carbon) to give Me<sub>2</sub>C=C=CMe<sub>2</sub>. Tetramethylallene (D<sub>2d</sub>) shows 3 <sup>13</sup>C lines (central sp C, equivalent terminal sp<sup>2</sup> C, equivalent CH<sub>3</sub>). From cyclooctene one obtains 9,9-dibromobicyclo[6.1.0]nonane and then cyclonona-1,2-diene. Each terminal allene carbon carries H and a ring CH<sub>2</sub>, so the allene axis is a chiral axis (axial chirality); the nine-membered ring keeps the two ends from interconverting easily, so the enantiomers do not racemise by simple bond rotation.")),
(57, ("(GATE/CSIR style) 2-Methylcyclohexanone tosylhydrazone is subjected to (A) 2.2 equiv <i>n</i>-BuLi, THF, 0 °C, then H<sub>2</sub>O (Shapiro), (B) the same sequence but quenched with DMF, and (C) NaOMe in ethylene glycol at 160 °C (protic Bamford–Stevens). Give the principal organic product in each case and the reason for the difference between A and C.",
      S([("A", "CC1CCCC=C1"), ("B", "O=CC1=CCCCC1C"), ("C", "CC1=CCCCC1")]),
      "A: 3-methylcyclohexene; B: 6-methylcyclohex-1-ene-1-carbaldehyde; C: 1-methylcyclohexene (major)",
      "Shapiro: the first equivalent of base deprotonates N–H, the second removes an α-proton under kinetic control, at the less hindered CH<sub>2</sub> (C-6) syn to the tosylamide anion. Loss of Ts<sup>−</sup> and N<sub>2</sub> gives the vinyllithium at C-1 (alkene C1=C6) which is protonated (A) or formylated by DMF (B). Protic Bamford–Stevens: the diazo compound is protonated to a diazonium ion; loss of N<sub>2</sub> gives a secondary cation which undergoes 1,2-H shift/E1 to the more substituted, thermodynamically favoured alkene 1-methylcyclohexene (C is usually a mixture with 3-methylcyclohexene, the ratio being conditions-dependent ⚑). The Shapiro reaction is kinetically controlled at carbon, the protic Bamford–Stevens reaction is cationic and thermodynamically controlled.")),
(58, ("(Multi-correct) Choose all correct statements about metal carbene complexes: (A) The carbene carbon of (CO)<sub>5</sub>Cr=C(OMe)Ph is electrophilic. (B) Schrock alkylidenes such as Ta(=CHCMe<sub>3</sub>)(CH<sub>2</sub>CMe<sub>3</sub>)<sub>3</sub> are typically early-metal, high-oxidation-state, and have a nucleophilic carbene carbon. (C) The α-CH<sub>3</sub> protons of (CO)<sub>5</sub>Cr=C(OMe)CH<sub>3</sub> are far more acidic (pK<sub>a</sub> ≈ 12) than those of methyl acetate. (D) A Fischer carbene is conveniently described as a singlet carbene donating a σ pair to the metal with some M→C π back-donation, whereas a Schrock alkylidene is a triplet fragment forming a covalent M=C bond. (E) The carbene carbon of Cp<sub>2</sub>Ta(=CH<sub>2</sub>)CH<sub>3</sub> is electrophilic. (F) Replacing OMe by NMe<sub>2</sub> in a Fischer carbene makes the carbene carbon more electrophilic.",
      None, "A, B, C, D",
      "(A) The heteroatom π-donates into the empty carbene p orbital but the electron-poor, π-accepting M(CO)<sub>5</sub> fragment keeps the carbon electrophilic (attacked by amines, hydride etc.). (B) Correct: Schrock alkylidenes react like phosphorus ylides (Wittig-type with carbonyls). (C) Deprotonation gives a metal-stabilised anion (charge delocalised onto the Cr(CO)<sub>5</sub> acceptor), so the α-H are acidic (pK<sub>a</sub> ≈ 12 in water, versus ≈ 25 for esters). (D) is the standard bonding picture. (E) is false because Schrock-type carbenes have a nucleophilic carbon. (F) is false: the more strongly π-donating NMe<sub>2</sub> reduces the electrophilicity of the carbene carbon. ⚑ Formal oxidation-state counting of Schrock carbenes (neutral CR<sub>2</sub> versus CR<sub>2</sub><sup>2−</sup> ligand) is convention-dependent, which does not affect the answers.")),
(59, ("(IChO-style, Chauvin) Norbornene (C<sub>7</sub>H<sub>10</sub>) reacts with a catalytic amount of Ru(=CHPh)Cl<sub>2</sub>(L)<sub>2</sub> in two different ways: (i) neat, concentrated: ring-opening metathesis polymerisation; (ii) in solution saturated with ethylene (1 atm): a single C<sub>9</sub>H<sub>14</sub> product. Give the repeating unit of (i), the structure and relative stereochemistry of (ii), and explain with the Chauvin mechanism why cyclohexene does not polymerise under the same conditions.",
      S([("norbornene", "C1CC2CC1C=C2"), ("(ii) product", "C=C[C@H]1CC[C@@H](C=C)C1")]),
      "(i) poly(1,3-cyclopentylenevinylene); (ii) <i>cis</i>-1,3-divinylcyclopentane (meso)",
      "Chauvin cycle: the Ru=CHR carbene undergoes [2+2] with the strained C=C to give a ruthenacyclobutane, whose retro-[2+2] opens the ring and regenerates a Ru alkylidene that is now attached to the cyclopentane chain; repeating this gives the polymer (units –CH=CH–C<sub>5</sub>H<sub>8</sub>–). With ethylene, Ru=CH<sub>2</sub> forms and cleaves the chain ends so that each ring-opened unit gets two vinyl groups (C<sub>7</sub>H<sub>10</sub> + C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>4</sub> = C<sub>9</sub>H<sub>14</sub>). The vinyl groups remain cis because they were the two bridgehead substituents on the same face of the bicycle; the product is the meso cis isomer, not the chiral trans pair. Ring-opening needs a thermodynamic driver: norbornene releases ≈ 27 kcal mol<sup>−1</sup> of strain, whereas cyclohexene has ≈ 1–2 kcal mol<sup>−1</sup>, and the entropy loss of polymerisation outweighs that, so no polymer forms.")),
(60, ("(IChO-style) Benzaldehyde and methyl vinyl ketone are stirred with 10 mol% 3-benzyl-5-(2-hydroxyethyl)-4-methylthiazolium chloride and Et<sub>3</sub>N in EtOH at 70 °C. (a) Give the product. (b) Draw the Breslow intermediate formed from benzaldehyde and the carbene. (c) Without MVK, what is formed, and why is the Stetter product favoured when MVK is present?",
      S([("Breslow intermediate (model, N-Me)", "OC(c1ccccc1)=C1SC=CN1C"), ("Stetter product", "CC(=O)CCC(=O)c1ccccc1")]),
      "(a) 1-phenylpentane-1,4-dione; (c) benzoin",
      "The base deprotonates the thiazolium C2–H (pK<sub>a</sub> ≈ 17–19) to give the N-heterocyclic carbene. The carbene adds to PhCHO to give the tetrahedral alkoxide, whose C–H proton transfers to give the enaminol (Breslow intermediate): an acyl-anion equivalent (umpolung). Without an acceptor, the Breslow intermediate attacks a second PhCHO (1,2-addition), then collapses to PhCOCH(OH)Ph (benzoin). Benzoin formation is reversible, whereas conjugate addition to the enone is irreversible and the enolate formed is protonated, after which the catalyst is expelled to give PhCO–CH<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>–COCH<sub>3</sub> (Stetter reaction). The Michael acceptor therefore drains the equilibrium toward the 1,4-diketone.")),
(61, ("(IChO-style) Crotyl phenyl sulfide (CH<sub>3</sub>CH=CHCH<sub>2</sub>SPh) reacts with ethyl diazoacetate in the presence of 1 mol% Rh<sub>2</sub>(OAc)<sub>4</sub>. (a) Give the product (Doyle–Kirmse reaction) and the key intermediate. (b) How many stereoisomers does the product have? (c) Why is the C=C bond not cyclopropanated?",
      S([("crotyl phenyl sulfide", "C/C=C/CSc1ccccc1"), ("product", "CCOC(=O)C(Sc1ccccc1)C(C)C=C")]),
      "ethyl 3-methyl-2-(phenylsulfanyl)pent-4-enoate; 4 stereoisomers (2 racemic diastereomers)",
      "The electrophilic Rh-carbenoid is attacked by the sulfur lone pair (the most nucleophilic site) to give a sulfonium ylide Ph–S<sup>+</sup>(CH<sub>2</sub>CH=CHCH<sub>3</sub>)–CH<sup>−</sup>CO<sub>2</sub>Et. This undergoes a [2,3]-sigmatropic rearrangement: the ylide carbon bonds to the γ-carbon (the CH–CH<sub>3</sub> terminus) while the S–CH<sub>2</sub> bond breaks, giving PhS–CH(CO<sub>2</sub>Et)–CH(CH<sub>3</sub>)–CH=CH<sub>2</sub>. Two new stereocentres (C-2, C-3) give 2<sup>2</sup> = 4 stereoisomers, i.e. two diastereomers each as a racemate. Ylide formation is faster than cyclopropanation of an unactivated alkene because the sulfide is a better Lewis base toward the carbenoid than the alkene.")),
(62, ("(IChO-style) 1-Diazo-5-methylhexan-2-one is treated with 1 mol% Rh<sub>2</sub>(OAc)<sub>4</sub> in CH<sub>2</sub>Cl<sub>2</sub>. (a) Give the major product and explain the regiochemistry in terms of ring size and C–H type. (b) The (<i>S</i>)-diazoketone N<sub>2</sub>CHCO(CH<sub>2</sub>)<sub>2</sub>CH(CH<sub>3</sub>)CH<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>3</sub> reacts the same way; assuming insertion with retention at the carbon that inserts, give the CIP descriptor of the product.",
      S([("(a) product", "CC1(C)CCC(=O)C1"), ("(b) substrate", "[N-]=[N+]=CC(=O)CC[C@@H](C)CC"), ("(b) product", "O=C1CC[C@@](C1)(C)CC")]),
      "(a) 3,3-dimethylcyclopentanone; (b) (<i>R</i>)-3-ethyl-3-methylcyclopentanone",
      "(a) The electrophilic Rh-carbenoid inserts into a C–H bond in a concerted, three-centre, hydride-transfer-like transition state. Insertion is fastest at the electron-rich tertiary C–H and a five-membered ring (1,5-insertion, favoured over 1,6) is formed, giving 3,3-dimethylcyclopentanone (not the six-membered 3-methylcyclohexanone that would arise from the primary methyl C–H). (b) The inserting carbon keeps its configuration (the new C–C bond forms on the face where H was), so the spatial arrangement is unchanged. The priorities change because the old H is replaced by a ring CH<sub>2</sub>, and the CIP label computed for the product is R (the label changes S → R although the geometry is retained). ⚑ Retention is the established stereochemical outcome for these insertions.")),
(63, ("(IChO-style, very hard) (a) (4-MeOC<sub>6</sub>H<sub>4</sub>)(C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>)C=CBr<sub>2</sub> is treated with 1 equiv <i>n</i>-BuLi at −78 °C and warmed to 20 °C (Fritsch–Buttenberg–Wiechell). Give the product. Why can the product not tell you whether anisyl or phenyl migrated? If the β-carbon is <sup>13</sup>C-labelled, what does a labelled carbon bonded to the phenyl ring tell you? (b) 3-Phenylpropanal is treated with the Ohira–Bestmann reagent (MeO)<sub>2</sub>P(O)C(N<sub>2</sub>)COCH<sub>3</sub> and K<sub>2</sub>CO<sub>3</sub> in MeOH. Give the product and the carbene involved, and the final step by which the alkyne forms.",
      S([("(a) product", "COc1ccc(C#Cc2ccccc2)cc1"), ("(b) product", "C#CCCc1ccccc1")]),
      "(a) 1-methoxy-4-(phenylethynyl)benzene; label on the carbon bonded to Ph means the anisyl group migrated; (b) 4-phenylbut-1-yne via alkylidene carbene and 1,2-H shift",
      "(a) Br/Li exchange gives the lithium carbenoid Ar(Ph)C=C(Li)Br; loss of LiBr gives the vinylidene (alkylidene carbene) Ar(Ph)C=C:, and a 1,2-aryl shift from Cβ to the carbene carbon gives ArC≡CPh. Whichever aryl group moves, the same alkyne results, so the product alone is uninformative. With <sup>13</sup>C at Cβ (the carbon that carries Ar and Ph before the shift): if anisyl migrates, Cβ keeps phenyl and the label sits on the alkyne carbon bonded to Ph; if phenyl migrates, the label sits next to the anisyl ring. (b) In MeOH, K<sub>2</sub>CO<sub>3</sub> cleaves the acetyl group to give the anion (MeO)<sub>2</sub>P(O)C(N<sub>2</sub>)<sup>−</sup>; it adds to the aldehyde and a Horner–Wadsworth–Emmons-type elimination gives the vinyl diazo compound RCH=CN<sub>2</sub>, which loses N<sub>2</sub> to give the alkylidene carbene RCH=C:. A 1,2-H shift from CH to the carbene carbon gives the terminal alkyne PhCH<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>C≡CH.")),
(64, ("(IChO-style, very hard) Pyrrole is treated with CHCl<sub>3</sub> and NaOEt/EtOH (Ciamician–Dennstedt). (a) Give the main ring-expanded product and the bicyclic intermediate. (b) If <sup>13</sup>CHCl<sub>3</sub> is used, which position of the product carries the label? (c) Indole reacts analogously: give the product. (d) What by-product is formed in Reimer–Tiemann conditions and why is it not the ring-expansion product?",
      S([("(a) product", "Clc1cccnc1"), ("(c) product", "Clc1cnc2ccccc2c1"), ("intermediate", "ClC1(Cl)C2C=CNC12")]),
      "(a) 3-chloropyridine; (b) C-3; (c) 3-chloroquinoline; (d) pyrrole-2-carbaldehyde",
      "Dichlorocarbene adds across C2=C3 of pyrrole to give 6,6-dichloro-2-azabicyclo[3.1.0]hex-3-ene (a gem-dichlorocyclopropane). Ionisation of C–Cl and disrotatory opening of the cyclopropyl cation (the cleavage of the old C2–C3 bond) insert the carbene carbon between old C2 and C3, with loss of HCl giving the aromatic ring, 3-chloropyridine. The labelled carbon becomes C-3 (it carries the retained Cl). Indole gives 3-chloroquinoline in the same manner. Under Reimer–Tiemann conditions (electrophilic substitution at C-2 by :CCl<sub>2</sub>/CCl<sub>3</sub><sup>−</sup> chemistry on the pyrrolide anion followed by hydrolysis), pyrrole-2-carbaldehyde is the competing product; the ring expansion is driven by ring strain release and aromatisation, while formylation requires proton loss at the intermediate stage.")),
(65, ("(IChO-style, very hard) 1-Diazohex-5-en-2-one, N<sub>2</sub>CHCO(CH<sub>2</sub>)<sub>2</sub>CH=CH<sub>2</sub>, is decomposed with Cu(acac)<sub>2</sub> in refluxing benzene. (a) Give the product and the number of stereoisomers. (b) Why is only the cis-fused ring system possible? (c) The product is then treated with Li / liquid NH<sub>3</sub> (then NH<sub>4</sub>Cl). Give the major product and state the stereoelectronic rule that decides which cyclopropane bond breaks.",
      S([("(a) product", "O=C1CCC2CC12"), ("(c) product", "CC1CCC(=O)C1")]),
      "(a) bicyclo[3.1.0]hexan-2-one, 2 stereoisomers (enantiomers); (c) 3-methylcyclopentanone",
      "(a) The copper carbenoid adds intramolecularly to the C5=C6 double bond: carbene C-1 binds to C-5 and C-6, forming a five-membered ring (C1, C2, C3, C4, C5) fused to a cyclopropane (C1, C5, C6). Two stereocentres (C-1, C-5) are present, but the molecule is chiral and cis fusion is the only possible geometry, so there are two enantiomers (racemic product with an achiral catalyst). (b) A trans-fused bicyclo[3.1.0]hexane would require extreme strain, and the tether (three atoms) makes a syn addition of the carbene across a Z-disposed ring bond only feasible cis. (c) Dissolving-metal reduction of the cyclopropyl ketone proceeds through a ketyl radical anion; the cyclopropane bond that overlaps best with the C=O π system breaks (Stork–Dauben rule), here the external C1–C6 bond rather than the internal C1–C5 bond, giving 3-methylcyclopentanone after protonation ⚑ (stereoelectronic selectivity; the alternative internal cleavage would give hexan-2-one).")),
(66, ("(IChO-style, very hard) 2-Diazo-1-tetralone (C<sub>10</sub>H<sub>8</sub>N<sub>2</sub>O) is irradiated in methanol. The product B (C<sub>11</sub>H<sub>12</sub>O<sub>2</sub>) has an IR band at 1735 cm<sup>−1</sup>, shows a 3H singlet at δ 3.7 and only 7 signals in its <sup>13</sup>C NMR spectrum. (a) Identify B and the ketene intermediate. (b) Show how the 13C count excludes the isomeric methyl indane-1-carboxylate. (c) What are the products when the same photolysis is run in water, and with 2-diazocyclohexanone in methanol?",
      S([("diazoketone", "O=C1C(=[N+]=[N-])CCc2ccccc12"), ("B", "COC(=O)C1Cc2ccccc2C1"), ("isomer excluded", "COC(=O)C1CCc2ccccc12")]),
      "B = methyl indane-2-carboxylate; ketene = indan-2-ylideneketene; excluded isomer has 11 signals",
      "Photolysis expels N<sub>2</sub> to give the α-ketocarbene at C-2, which undergoes Wolff rearrangement: the aryl carbon attached to C-1 migrates to the carbene carbon (C-2), giving the ring-contracted ketene (indan-2-ylideneketene; the former C=O carbon becomes the ketene carbon). Methanol adds to give methyl indane-2-carboxylate. This molecule has a mirror plane, so its <sup>13</sup>C spectrum shows 3 aromatic + CH<sub>2</sub> (2C) + CH + C=O + OCH<sub>3</sub> = 7 signals. Methyl indane-1-carboxylate would show 6 aromatic + 3 aliphatic ring carbons + C=O + OCH<sub>3</sub> = 11 signals. In water the product is indane-2-carboxylic acid; 2-diazocyclohexanone in MeOH gives methyl cyclopentanecarboxylate (via cyclopentylideneketene).")),
(67, ("(IChO-style, very hard) Benzaldehyde-<i>d</i><sub>1</sub> (PhCDO, &gt; 99% D) is converted to benzoin with a thiazolium salt/Et<sub>3</sub>N in EtOH. (a) Where does the deuterium appear in the product (<sup>1</sup>H NMR: no singlet at δ ≈ 5.95; mass spectrum of M = 213)? (b) Labelled benzoin PhCO–CD(OH)Ph is then stirred with <i>p</i>-anisaldehyde (ArCHO) and fresh catalyst; ignoring minor processes, predict the isotopic composition of the two crossed benzoins PhCO–CH(OH)Ar and ArCO–CH(OH)Ph that appear first (and which is labelled). (c) Explain how the mechanism accounts for (a) and for (b).",
      None, "(a) D on the carbinol carbon only: PhCO–CD(OH)Ph; (b) PhCO–CH(OH)Ar is unlabelled; ArCO–CD(OH)Ph carries the D",
      "(a) Addition of the carbene to PhCDO gives a tetrahedral alkoxide; the former aldehyde C–D proton is transferred to O to give the Breslow enaminol Ph–C(OD)=C(cat). The acyl carbon of the product comes from this unit, so it carries no D; the O–D exchanges with EtOH. The enaminol attacks a second molecule of PhCDO, whose D stays on the new carbinol carbon, giving PhCO–CD(OH)Ph (M = 213; the carbinol C–H signal is absent). (b) Benzoin formation is reversible: the carbene adds to the ketone of PhCO–CD(OH)Ph and the C–C bond breaks to give the Breslow intermediate of the PhCO unit and releases the carbinol fragment as PhCDO. The Ph–C(OH)=cat unit adds to ArCHO to give PhCO–CH(OH)Ar (no D), while the released PhCDO is picked up by the anisoyl Breslow intermediate to give ArCO–CD(OH)Ph (labelled). The D never moves to the acyl carbon in either direction.")),
(68, ("(IChO-style, very hard) Cr(CO)<sub>5</sub>=C(OMe)Ph is heated with 3-hexyne in THF (60 °C), then the mixture is exposed to air or oxidised. (a) Give the organic product (Dötz benzannulation). (b) With 1-hexyne as the alkyne, which regioisomer is formed? (c) Which atoms of the product come from the carbene carbon, one CO ligand and the aryl ring?",
      S([("(a) product", "CCc1c(CC)c(O)c2ccccc2c1OC"), ("(b) product", "CCCCc1cc(OC)c2ccccc2c1O")]),
      "(a) 2,3-diethyl-4-methoxynaphthalen-1-ol; (b) 2-butyl-4-methoxynaphthalen-1-ol",
      "Sequence: CO loss, alkyne insertion into the Cr=C bond, CO insertion to give a vinylketene–Cr complex, electrocyclisation onto the ortho position of the phenyl ring, and tautomerisation to the aromatic naphthol, which is the Cr(CO)<sub>3</sub> complex that is freed on oxidation. The carbene carbon becomes C-4 (it bears OMe), the CO becomes C-1 (it bears OH, para to OMe), the alkyne carbons become C-2 and C-3, and the phenyl ring supplies C-4a to C-8a (the benzo ring). With 1-hexyne the larger butyl group ends up adjacent to the phenolic oxygen (C-2), giving 2-butyl-4-methoxynaphthalen-1-ol. ⚑ This regiochemistry is the usual steric outcome and weaker for electronically biased alkynes. Oxidative work-up (CAN) of such 4-methoxy-1-naphthols gives 1,4-naphthoquinones.")),
]
